Late Bronze Age era (circa 1600-1100 BCE), and it played a role in the Trojan 
war as recorded in Homer’s Iliad and Odyssey epic poems. It was the seat of the 
ruler, the economic engine, and the administrative center of the mainly 
agricultural activities of the region. The palace is associated with the 
legendary King (wanax) Nestor, a figure from the Homer’s epic poems known for 
his wisdom and experience in war. It was built by Neleus, the father of Nestor 
and founder of the Neleid dynasty (early 13th c. In the Late Helladic period the
area was occupied by many houses that were built on the acropolis and on the 
lowest terraces of the hill, but after a fire destroyed them, the “wanax” 
