    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Arthur Evans’s 1925 Journal of Hellenic Studies 
article presents the Ring of Nestor as a gold signet ring forming part of a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals said to come from a 
burial context at Thisbê in Boeotia. Evans uses the ring’s imagery, which he 
describes in detail as showing figures, divinities and ritual scenes, to propose
an interpretation in terms of a Minoan conception of the afterlife and links it 
to ideas about early Aegean funerary practice and religion. He assigns the ring 
to the Late Bronze Age Aegean, within a Minoan–Mycenaean cultural milieu, and 
treats it as a representative and particularly important work for understanding 
prehistoric Greek religious iconography and the ‘after-world’.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The 1977 Nestor Bibliography of Aegean and Related 
Areas lists Arthur Evans’s study of the Ring of Nestor as an important reference
under the heading of prehistoric Greek beliefs about the afterlife. The entry 
identifies the original publication in the Journal of Hellenic Studies and notes
that the version referred to in the bibliography is abridged from that article. 
By cataloguing this work, the bibliography shows that the Ring of Nestor is 
treated in scholarship as a notable gold signet ring associated with the 
‘after-world’ of prehistoric Greece and situates it within Aegean archaeology 
and the study of religion.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "The Ashmolean Museum publication on Arthur Evans and 
The Palace of Minos includes a section on the Ring of Nestor. It describes the 
Ring of Nestor as a gold signet ring first described and named by archaeologist 
Arthur Evans and connects it to his wider reconstruction of Minoan and early 
Greek religious imagery. The text indicates that the ring’s authenticity and the
circumstances of its discovery have been questioned since it entered scholarly 
discussion, suggesting doubts about its findspot and excavation context. It 
further implies that Evans acquired the ring through his collecting activities 
rather than through controlled archaeological excavation, and notes its role as 
a key object in Evans’s visualisation of Minoan religious scenes.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "The article on the Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos 
discusses Mycenaean gold rings from a well-documented burial and briefly 
references the Ring of Nestor as a comparative example. It states that the Ring 
of Nestor was purchased by Arthur Evans and is said to have been found to the 
north of Pylos, while emphasising that this supposed provenance is uncertain. By
contrasting the secure archaeological context of the Griffin Warrior’s gold 
rings with the ambiguous origin of the Ring of Nestor, the article highlights 
the latter’s unclear discovery circumstances and uses its story to illustrate 
older, poorly documented finds of Mycenaean-type signet rings in the Pylos 
region.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "The Metropolitan Museum of Art article on historic 
images of the Greek Bronze Age surveys widely reproduced visual documents that 
shaped early twentieth-century perceptions of Aegean prehistory. It cites the 
Ring of Nestor alongside the Phaistos Disk as a typical object whose photographs
and drawings were extensively circulated, helping construct the public and 
scholarly visual imagination of Bronze Age Greece. The text notes that such 
images were repeatedly used in publications and exhibitions, and situates the 
Ring of Nestor within a broader corpus of iconic artifacts, including 
wall-paintings like the nautilus frieze from Pylos, that allowed comparative 
aesthetic and thematic studies.",
