["Identification of one core scholarly article (Davis & Stocker, \"The Lord of 
the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos\" and its academia.edu version) 
that provides the broader iconographic and archaeological context for Pylian 
gold rings, including the later so‑called \"Ring of Nestor\".", "Documentation 
of Davis & Stocker’s interpretation that concentrations of gold signet rings 
signal special religious‑political status (warrior–priest or royal roles) and 
their use in funerary, ancestor cult, and kingship rituals aimed at legitimizing
emerging Mycenaean rulers.", "Evidence that the iconography on the Griffin 
Warrior rings is closely related to Cretan Minoan seal traditions and carries 
ritual, religious, and power connotations, showing active Mycenaean adoption and
transformation of Minoan imagery.", "Claims and supporting statements that the 
Griffin Warrior rings and comparable western Messenian grave assemblages form 
part of a shared elite material culture that constructs trans‑regional identity,
which frames how the Pylos gold rings (including the later \"Ring of Nestor\") 
are studied.", "Explicit recognition that Davis & Stocker’s work supplies 
tables, references, and glyptic background that later scholarship uses when 
discussing the Pylos gold ring termed the \"Ring of Nestor\", while noting that 
this study itself does not resolve debates over that specific ring’s function or
its Homeric association."], "requirement": "Collect evidence for this Query 
only. Do not broaden into sibling topics."}
