    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Joan Aruz’s 2024 paper \"Demeter & Persephone at 
Mycenaean Pylos\" discusses religious imagery and cult at Mycenaean Pylos, 
focusing on cults identified with Demeter and Persephone or related divine 
figures. In this context it examines Pylian glyptic, including gold signet 
rings, and links them to deities such as a goddess designated U-po-jo. The study
cites Jack L. Davis and Sharon R. Stocker’s 2016 article \"The Lord of the Gold 
Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos\" as a key reference for the interpretation 
of gold rings and uses their framework of elite religious and ideological 
significance. Within this broader discussion, the so‑called Ring of Nestor is 
treated as part of a set of elite glyptic objects that participate in ritual and
cultic practices at Pylos, and its iconography is interpreted in relation to 
local cults of Demeter and Persephone.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The edited volume \"Current Approaches and New 
Perspectives in Aegean Iconography\" (Aegis 18) includes A. G. Vlachopoulos’s 
chapter \"The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity\". This chapter 
re‑examines the so‑called Ring of Nestor, addressing long‑standing issues of its
authenticity, provenance, and iconographic interpretation within Aegean 
iconography. Vlachopoulos situates the study within many years of research on 
the ring (published in CMS VI) and engages with both early 20th‑century 
treatments and recent scholarship. The chapter cites Davis and Stocker’s \"The 
Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos\" as part of the contextual
framework for Pylian gold rings and the Griffin Warrior assemblage but shifts 
the focus toward evaluating the material’s authenticity and the historiography 
of its interpretation. The treatment of the Ring of Nestor critically revisits 
its traditional Homeric associations and earlier ideological or ritual readings,
positioning the ring within debates over genuine versus forged artifacts and 
offering updated perspectives on its imagery and status.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "A. G. Vlachopoulos’s 2020 chapter \"The Ring of 
