  "documents": [
    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Florian Hudler’s 2019 paper discusses Aegean 
forgeries and problematic artefacts, focusing on how digital reproduction and 
circulation of images affect debates on authenticity and scholarly 
interpretation. The ‘Ring of Nestor’ is presented as a paradigmatic case of a 
gemma dubitanda, whose archaeological provenance, discovery context, and 
collection history are uncertain or contested. Hudler reviews the ring’s 
supposed origin near Pylos, its large gold signet form, and the complex 
multi-figure relief scene it carries, and compares its iconography to securely 
provenanced Minoan and Mycenaean gold rings. The article traces the history of 
scholarly arguments for and against the ring’s authenticity, including 
stylistic, iconographic, and preservation-based arguments, and shows how the 
ring has been repeatedly used as evidence for Mycenaean religious and political 
symbolism despite doubts about its status. Hudler argues that the digital and 
scholarly reproduction of such contested images shapes understandings of 
Mycenaean elite religious practice and visual politics, independent of the 
artefacts’ archaeological reliability.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The Oxford Journal of Archaeology article by Ingo 
Pini titled ‘The “Ring of Nestor”’ (1998) offers a comprehensive study of the 
Pylos gold signet ring catalogued as CMS VI, no. 277. Pini describes the ring’s 
physical characteristics, including its large size, gold material, manufacturing
technique, and detailed relief imagery, and situates it among Cretan and 
Mycenaean gold signet rings. The study reconstructs the ring’s reported 
discovery near Pylos and its early collecting history, emphasizing the lack of 
secure excavation records and assessing how this affects its use as 
archaeological evidence. Through iconographic analysis, Pini discusses the 
arrangement of divine and human figures and architectural or natural elements on
the bezel, evaluating interpretations that see a theophany, sacrificial ritual, 
or confirmation of royal power, and comparing the composition to Minoan epiphany
rings such as the Isopata ring. The article considers the ring’s possible 
function as a symbol of authority and religious signet used in elite funerary or
cult contexts and weighs arguments about its authenticity versus modern forgery,
offering a cautious judgment that informs later debates on Mycenaean religion 
and political symbolism.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "Kyria Tamvaki’s study on ‘Minoan and Mycenaean 
Elements in the Iconography of the Pylos Sealings’ examines the imagery on 
administrative sealings and related seals, including gold rings, from Pylos. The
work distinguishes motifs and stylistic traits derived from Minoan 
traditions—such as goddess figures and nature-centred cult scenes—from those 
that reflect local Mycenaean developments. Within this framework, Tamvaki 
discusses certain gold rings associated with Pylos, including the piece later 
termed the ‘Ring of Nestor’, highlighting scenes that show female divinities, 
epiphany-like appearances, processions, or elite gatherings akin to Cretan 
epiphany rings. The chapter argues that such images played a role in religious 
ritual and in expressing symbols of authority at the Pylian palace and among 
local elites. By situating the ring within the broader sealing system and 
glyptic repertoire at Pylos, Tamvaki presents it as part of a wider 
Creto-Mycenaean iconographic tradition used in administrative practice and in 
constructing religious and political legitimacy.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "The book chapter on ‘Cretomycenaean glyptic in LM 
II/LH IIB–IIIA1’ surveys gold rings and other seals that exemplify the mixed 
Cretan and Mycenaean styles of the later Late Minoan II and early Late Helladic 
IIB–IIIA1 periods. Within this overview, the author treats the ‘Ring of Nestor’ 
(CMS VI, no. 277) as a key example of a Creto-Mycenaean gold signet. The chapter
analyses the ring’s form, composition, and carving technique, and compares its 
imagery—featuring a goddess, worshippers, and cult architecture or symbolic 
structures—to other LM II–III gold rings from Crete and the mainland. On 
stylistic and iconographic grounds, the ring is assigned to the LM II/LH 
IIB–IIIA1 horizon, with discussion of how its ‘Cretan’ features coexist with 
Mycenaean elements. The author explores the ring’s likely function as a signet 
of authority used by high-status individuals and its potential role in ritual 
acts, including sealing practices linked to cult and political activities. The 
ring is placed within the wider elite material culture of Pylos and western 
Messenia, where imported and adapted Cretan religious imagery was used by early 
Mycenaean rulers to articulate royal ideology and legitimacy.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "The study ‘Demeter & Persephone at Mycenaean Pylos’, 
circulated via ResearchGate, investigates cults of goddesses at Mycenaean Pylos 
with particular attention to underworld and seasonal mythological themes. One 
section focuses on the gold ‘Ring of Nestor’, reproduced as Figure 14 and 
captioned as depicting a goddess identified with the Linear B deity 
u-po-jo-po-ti-ni-ja in an underworld setting dated broadly to ca. 1700–1450 BCE.
The author correlates features of the ring’s imagery—such as the posture of the 
central female figure, associated symbols, and environmental motifs—with texts 
and mythological models related to Demeter and Persephone. The ring is 
interpreted as visual evidence for an underworld or chthonic manifestation of a 
Pylian goddess, supporting arguments about ancestor cult, the veneration of 
subterranean deities, and cyclical fertility narratives at Pylos. The study 
proposes that elite use and display of such a gold ring contributed to 
religious–political strategies whereby ruling or priestly figures asserted 
special connections to powerful goddesses of the underworld, thereby reinforcing
their authority within the Mycenaean sociopolitical system.",
      "source_ref": "s5"
    }
  ],
