  "documents": [
    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Vlachopoulos 2020 systematically re-examines the 
so‑called \"Ring of Nestor\" in terms of authenticity, provenance, style and 
iconography. He describes the ring as a large gold signet ring (3.29 × 2.18 cm),
among the largest Aegean gold signet rings, and argues on technical and 
stylistic grounds that it is a genuine Late Bronze Age artefact rather than a 
modern forgery. The study surveys early scholarly doubts, especially from 
Nilsson and Biesantz, who suspected the ring (together with the so‑called \"Ring
of Minos\") of being a forgery possibly connected with the Gilliéron workshop, 
and responds by emphasising congruence with Mycenaean goldworking and 
comparanda. The chapter reconstructs the historiography of the ring's naming and
dissemination: Evans and Gilliéron redrew and publicised the ring, and the 
designation \"Ring of Nestor\" arose from modern scholarly and popular attempts 
to associate a gold signet ring allegedly from a large beehive tomb at Pylos 
with the Homeric king Nestor, in the absence of any ancient inscription. 
Vlachopoulos carries out an iconographic analysis, linking the ring's imagery to
Mycenaean religious practice and royal ideology, including elite rituals and 
kingship symbolism. He concludes that, while the Homeric name is a modern 
convention and does not prove an historical connection to Nestor, if authentic 
the ring nonetheless offers indirect evidence about the ideological and 
religious context later reflected in Homeric depictions of kingship.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The 2020 issue of the Nestor Bibliography of Aegean 
and Related Areas includes a formal bibliographic entry for A. G. Vlachopoulos's
chapter \"The Ring of Nestor and the Quest for Authenticity\" in the volume 
Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography (Aegis 18). The 
entry gives the chapter's page range (223–252) and situates it within studies of
Late Bronze Age Aegean iconography and religion. An accompanying descriptive 
note indicates that the chapter focuses on the authenticity of the \"Ring of 
Nestor\" and its position within Aegean iconographic research, addressing its 
naming, supposed find context, and the interpretation of its imagery. The 
bibliography categorises the work under Late Bronze Age religious iconography, 
signalling its relevance to discussions of Mycenaean cult and royal symbolism, 
and it highlights the chapter as a key recent reference on debates over the 
ring's authenticity and name.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "In a chapter of Knossos and the Prophets of 
Modernism, Cathy Gere discusses controversies surrounding Arthur Evans, his 
collaborators Emile and Georges Gilliéron, and accusations of forgery in 
relation to Minoan and Mycenaean artefacts. The so‑called \"Ring of Nestor\" is 
used as a case study: Gere notes stylistic anomalies and problems in the 
reported discovery narrative that led some scholars to suspect the ring might be
a later \"pseudo-antique\" rather than a genuine Late Bronze Age artefact. 
Drawing on Nilsson and later critics, she reports that both the \"Ring of 
Minos\" and \"Ring of Nestor\" have been proposed as possible creations of the 
Gilliéron workshop. Gere argues that the heroic names attached to these objects 
catered to early twentieth‑century desires to link archaeological finds directly
to Homeric and legendary figures. She emphasises that the widespread acceptance 
of the \"Ring of Nestor\" owed much to Evans's authority and his visual and 
narrative construction of a Mycenaean–Homeric continuum, rather than to robust 
archaeological documentation. Consequently, she treats the name \"Ring of 
Nestor\" as a modern, symbolically loaded label and warns against using the 
object uncritically as evidence for the historical reality of Homeric Nestor.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "Palmer's 2014 PhD thesis on Late Bronze Age Aegean 
glyptic motifs of religious character includes discussion of disputed artefacts,
notably the so‑called \"Ring of Minos\" (AM 1938.1110) and \"Ring of Nestor\" 
(VI.277). She cites Nilsson (1950: 42–49) and Biesantz (1954: 115–119), who had 
denounced these rings as fakes, and records that both rings have long been 
suspected of modern manufacture. Palmer notes that naming such objects after 
legendary or Homeric figures like Minos and Nestor reflects an early 
twentieth‑century tendency among scholars and collectors to seek direct Homeric 
connections in material remains, rather than being grounded in inscriptions or 
secure evidence of ownership. Because of ongoing doubts about authenticity, she 
excludes these controversial rings from her statistical analysis of religious 
glyptic motifs, in order to avoid skewing the sample with possible modern 
forgeries. Nevertheless, she briefly summarises later scholarship that has 
argued in favour of their authenticity and observes that, if genuine, the \"Ring
of Nestor\" might depict a religious scene linked to kingship and cult, which 
could be viewed as indirectly resonant with Homeric portrayals of Nestor's royal
and ritual roles.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "In Minoan-Mycenaean Religion and Its Survival in 
Greek Religion (1927), Martin P. Nilsson addresses problems of forgery and 
authenticity among Minoan and Mycenaean artefacts. He explicitly refers to the 
so‑called \"Ring of Nestor\" and suggests that it and certain comparable pieces 
\"seem rather to be modern products\". Nilsson argues this on stylistic and 
technical grounds, claiming that the workmanship and style differ from those of 
securely provenanced Mycenaean signet rings, and he hints at a date of 
manufacture in the modern period. He also stresses that the discovery 
circumstances and subsequent transmission of the \"Ring of Nestor\" as reported 
by Evans lack solid, independently verifiable archaeological documentation, 
thereby undermining any firm link to Mycenaean Pylos or to a historical Nestor. 
Nilsson expresses scepticism toward the practice of naming artefacts after 
mythic or Homeric figures, warning that such labels project romantic narratives 
onto prehistoric religious material and may mislead readers regarding historical
connections.",
      "source_ref": "s5"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d6",
      "document_summary": "A modern historiographical study on Arthur Evans and 
Minoan Crete, published under the title Sir Arthur Evans and Minoan Crete: 
Creating the Vision of Knossos, examines how Evans constructed visual and 
narrative representations of Knossos and related sites. In this context, the 
so‑called \"Ring of Nestor\" is discussed. The text notes that Evans drew the 
ring in his Knossos notebook (Ashmolean Museum) and recounts his version of its 
discovery in a large beehive tomb at \"Nestor's Pylos\". The author analyses how
Evans used drawings, exhibitions and evocative naming strategies to link 
specific artefacts, such as gold signet rings, with heroic figures like Minos 
and Nestor and thereby reinforce an impression of continuity between Mycenaean 
archaeology and Homeric epic. The discussion suggests that such narrative naming
practices belong to a modern interpretive framework rather than reflecting the 
self-identification of the artefacts' ancient owners. The treatment of the 
\"Ring of Nestor\" illustrates how its Homeric designation originated and spread
through Evans's influential reconstructions, rather than through direct 
archaeological or textual evidence.",
      "source_ref": "s6"
    }
  ],
