\"Ring of Minos\" and the \"Ring of Nestor\" could have been produced in the 
Gilliéron workshop.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "qualifiers": [
        "attributing_view_to_other_scholars"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st12",
      "text": "Gere argues that naming artefacts as the \"Ring of Minos\" or 
\"Ring of Nestor\" catered to early twentieth‑century desires to establish 
direct links between archaeological objects and Homeric or legendary figures.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "qualifiers": [
        "interpretive"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st13",
      "text": "Gere maintains that the diffusion and acceptance of the \"Ring of
Nestor\" as an iconic object owed more to Evans's authority and his construction
of a visual narrative of Mycenaean–Homeric continuity than to rigorous 
archaeological documentation.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "qualifiers": [
        "interpretive"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st14",
      "text": "Gere treats the label \"Ring of Nestor\" as a modern, 
symbolically charged construct and cautions against using the ring uncritically 
as evidence for the historical existence of Homeric Nestor.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "qualifiers": [
        "interpretive",
        "critical_of_Homeric_association"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st15",
      "text": "Palmer records that the so‑called \"Ring of Minos\" (AM 
1938.1110) and \"Ring of Nestor\" (VI.277) were denounced as fakes by Nilsson 
(1950: 42–49) and Biesantz (1954: 115–119).",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "qualifiers": [
        "reporting_historical_opinion"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st16",
      "text": "Palmer states that naming controversial rings after figures such 
as Minos and Nestor reflects a broader early twentieth‑century tendency to seek 
direct Homeric connections in material culture rather than being based on 
inscriptions or demonstrable ownership.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "qualifiers": [
        "interpretive"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st17",
      "text": "Due to doubts about authenticity, Palmer excludes the \"Ring of 
Nestor\" from her statistical analysis of Late Bronze Age religious glyptic 
motifs to avoid including possible modern forgeries.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "qualifiers": [
        "methodological_decision"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st18",
      "text": "Palmer notes that subsequent scholarship has argued in favour of 
the authenticity of the \"Ring of Nestor\" and remarks that, if genuine, its 
imagery might depict a religious scene associated with kingship and cult that 
could be indirectly compared with Homeric portrayals of Nestor's royal role.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "qualifiers": [
        "interpretive",
        "conditional_on_authenticity"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st19",
      "text": "Nilsson asserts that the so‑called \"Ring of Nestor\" appears to 
be a modern product rather than a genuine Mycenaean artefact, based on perceived
stylistic and technical discrepancies with securely provenanced Mycenaean signet
rings.",
      "document_ref": "d5",
      "source_ref": "s5",
      "qualifiers": [
        "authenticity_assessment"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st20",
      "text": "Nilsson argues that the reported discovery circumstances and 
subsequent transmission of the \"Ring of Nestor\" as presented by Evans lack 
solid, independently verifiable archaeological documentation.",
      "document_ref": "d5",
      "source_ref": "s5",
      "qualifiers": [
        "critique_of_provenance"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st21",
      "text": "Nilsson criticises the practice of naming prehistoric artefacts 
after mythic or Homeric figures, contending that such labels project romantic 
narratives onto the material and risk misleading readers about historical 
connections.",
      "document_ref": "d5",
      "source_ref": "s5",
      "qualifiers": [
        "interpretive"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st22",
      "text": "The study Sir Arthur Evans and Minoan Crete reports that Evans 
drew the \"Ring of Nestor\" in his Knossos notebook (now in the Ashmolean 
Museum) and that he claimed the ring was found in a large beehive tomb at 
\"Nestor's Pylos\".",
      "document_ref": "d6",
      "source_ref": "s6",
      "qualifiers": [
        "based_on_Evans_notebooks"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st23",
      "text": "The same study argues that Evans used drawings, displays and 
evocative naming strategies to connect specific artefacts, including gold rings 
like the \"Ring of Nestor\", with heroic figures such as Minos and Nestor, 
thereby promoting a perceived continuity between Mycenaean archaeology and 
Homeric epic.",
      "document_ref": "d6",
      "source_ref": "s6",
      "qualifiers": [
        "interpretive"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "st24",
      "text": "The discussion in Sir Arthur Evans and Minoan Crete suggests that
the Homeric association implied by the name \"Ring of Nestor\" stems from modern
interpretive frameworks devised by Evans and his contemporaries rather than from
ancient evidence about the ring's owner or use.",
      "document_ref": "d6",
      "source_ref": "s6",
      "qualifiers": [
        "interpretive"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "medium"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
