["Evans’s 1925 account presents the Ring of Nestor as part of a sepulchral 
treasure from a burial at Thisbê in Boeotia, whereas later sources linked to 
Evans’s collecting and later scholarship describe the ring as purchased by Evans
with a supposedly northern Pylos provenance and emphasize uncertainty about its 
discovery context and authenticity.", "There is a conflict between Hudler’s 
classification of the ‘Ring of Nestor’ as a gemma dubitanda with problematic 
provenance and authenticity, and other scholars’ treatments that implicitly or 
explicitly accept the ring as an authentic Late Bronze Age artefact used for 
