"Bibliographic treatment in the 1977 Nestor Bibliography confirms Evans’s JHS 
article and its abridged reuse, locating it within Aegean archaeology and 
afterlife studies.", "Later scholarly/overview sources (Ashmolean Museum 
publication, Met Museum perspectives piece, a technological study of Mycenaean 
gold objects, and the Griffin Warrior article) are located and shown to 
reference Evans’s study and the Ring of Nestor.", "These secondary sources 
collectively describe the ring’s problematic provenance and absence from modern 
stratigraphically recorded excavations, meeting the criterion that there is no 
modern excavation report for the ring itself.", "Evans’s own narrative is 
documented: he presents the ring as part of a sepulchral treasure from a burial 
at Thisbê in Boeotia and uses that reported context for interpretation.", "Later
sources (Ashmolean, Griffin Warrior article) explicitly state that the ring was 
acquired through Evans’s collecting, was purchased rather than excavated, and 
has a ‘supposed’ findspot north of Pylos, with emphasis on uncertainty.", "The 
evidence establishes that there is no secure, modern excavation context at Pylos
for the Ring of Nestor; instead there are conflicting, poorly documented claimed
findspots (Thisbê burial versus ‘north of Pylos’).", "Some contextual details 
about the ring’s role in scholarship (iconic status, use as comparative example 
in technological studies, its impact on visual culture) are documented."], 
