2019 paper on Aegean gemmae dubitandae, which uses the ring as a central case.",
"For Pini 1998, the document summary shows that the article’s primary focus is 
the Ring of Nestor: it describes its physical characteristics (large gold 
signet, manufacture, detailed relief), analyses its imagery (arrangement of 
divine and human figures, architectural/natural elements; epiphany, sacrificial 
rite, or royal‑authority ceremony; comparison with Isopata and other epiphany 
rings), reconstructs its reported discovery near Pylos and early collecting 
history, emphasizes the lack of secure excavation records, evaluates 
authenticity, and discusses its possible function as a symbol of authority and 
religious signet in elite funerary or cult contexts.", "For the Cretomycenaean 
glyptic chapter, the summary indicates that the Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277)
is explicitly singled out as a paradigmatic Creto‑Mycenaean gold signet: the 
chapter analyses its form, composition, carving technique, and imagery (goddess,
worshippers, cult architecture), assigns it stylistically to LM II/LH IIB–IIIA1,
and discusses its likely function as a high‑status signet used in administrative
sealing and ritual, and its role in articulating royal ideology and political 
legitimacy in Pylos/western Messenia.", "For Tamvaki’s study on Pylos sealings, 
the document summary shows that the work treats gold rings associated with 
Pylos, explicitly including the later so‑called Ring of Nestor, distinguishing 
Minoan‑derived goddess/epiphany/nature cult scenes from local Mycenaean 
elements. It discusses ring scenes of female divinity, epiphany‑like 
appearances, processions, and elite gatherings analogous to Cretan epiphany 
rings, and argues that such imagery functioned within palace and elite contexts 
to express religious ritual and authority, thereby contributing to political 
legitimacy.", "Hudler’s peer‑reviewed paper is documented as using the Ring of 
Nestor as a paradigmatic case of a gemma dubitanda: it reviews the ring’s 
supposed origin near Pylos, large gold signet form, complex multi‑figure 
religious scene, and collection history; surveys arguments for and against 
authenticity; stresses its problematic provenance; and shows how, despite these 
issues, scholars repeatedly use its imagery as evidence for Mycenaean religious 
practice and elite power.", "Across these studies, the core acceptance criteria 
are met: each identified item clearly (a) discusses the ring’s imagery in detail
(goddess, worshippers, epiphany/ritual/underworld scenes, cult architecture); 
(b) addresses its archaeological provenance or lack thereof (reported Pylos 
origin, absence of secure excavation records, classification as gemma 
dubitanda); and (c) proposes functions and meanings for the ring within 
Mycenaean (or Creto‑Mycenaean) religious and political practice (personal signet
of authority, elite funerary/cult object, participation in administrative 
sealing and ritual, instrument of royal ideology, visual claim to chthonic 
goddess connections)."], "requirement": "Collect evidence for this Query only. 
Do not broaden into sibling topics."}
