nianmag-media.s3.amazonaws.com/filer/50/00/50004a10-2d2c-4380-849d-9d83856edfcb/
man_on_stake.jpg)\n\nSome 8,000 years ago, the skull of a Scandinavian man in 
his 50s was impaled on a wooden stake in Sweden. Now, a new facial 
reconstruction by Swedish forensic artist [Oscar 
Nilsson](https://www.odnilsson.com/) allows modern viewers to envision this 
mysterious individual’s prominent cheekbones, blue eyes and brown hair, reports 
Kristin Romey for [*National 
Geographic*](https://www.nationalgeographic.com/history/2020/06/exclusive-skull-
ritual-site-motala-reconstructed/).\n\nArchaeologists found the man’s cranium—as
well as the skulls of eight other adults and one infant—in the boggy sediment of
the Kanaljorden excavation site in Motala, Sweden, in 2012. The discovery marked
the first archaeological evidence of Mesolithic hunter-gatherers [mounting human
skulls on stakes](https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.210).\n\nKanaljorden is of 
particular interest because the remains found there were arranged in such an 
uncommon, purposeful way, according to Elaina Zachos of *[*National 
Geographic*](https://www.nationalgeographic.com/news/2018/02/human-skulls-mounte
d-on-stakes-river-mystery-mesolithic-sweden-spd/)*. The human burial, which also
included animal bones, was placed on a stone platform submerged in the middle of
a small lake. Researchers found remnants of wooden stakes inside the skulls of 
two of the men.\n\nTo give one of these ancient humans—a man nicknamed 
“Ludvig”—a face, Nilsson scanned his skull and created a 3-D plastic replica of 
it, reports George Dvorsky for 
[*Gizmodo*](https://gizmodo.com/facial-reconstruction-of-a-prehistoric-man-whose
-head-w-1844179970). The forensic artist then drew on genetic evidence to 
capture Ludvig’s pale complexion and hair and eye color. But the biggest 
challenge stemmed from what the skull lacked.\n\n![Facial reconstruction of 
Ludvig](https://th-thumbnailer.cdn-si-edu.com/wkuo-fsDbVlZHAANmNV8GRcn_q8=/fit-i
n/1072x0/filters:focal(1227x979:1228x980)/https://tf-cmsv2-smithsonianmag-media.
s3.amazonaws.com/filer/91/b1/91b102c3-8b03-4516-8371-40008708086a/img_5120.jpg)\
n\n“The jaw was missing, so I had to calculate and reconstruct it from studying 
and measuring the skull,” Nilsson tells *Smithsonian* magazine via email. “This 
is time consuming and difficult, [and] the error margin of course gets bigger 
when such a vital part is missing.”\n\nThough the majority of the adult 
Mesolithic skulls in the grave had no jawbones, a [2018 
paper](https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.210) on the find detailed the presence 
of jawbones from various animals, including bears, wild boars, deer, moose and 
badgers.\n\nThe wild boar remains inspired Nilsson to give Ludvig a cloak made 
of the animal’s hide.\n\n“He wears the skin from a wild boar,” explains Nilsson 
to [*Live 
Science*](https://www.livescience.com/stone-age-man-facial-reconstruction.html)’
s Laura Geggel. *“*We can see from how the human skulls and animal jaws were 
found that they clearly meant a big deal in their cultural and 
