initially unwary approach to the object as an authentic Bronze Age artifact and 
now discussed it, among other examples, as a ‘suspect object.’39 This revision 
was a necessary step considering the increasing pressure that is perceptible in 
comments on the book’s first edition: many reviews brought up the problem of 
potential fakes among his objects of study at a time when general awareness for 
this matter had risen.40 In 1954, Hagen Biesantz took a decisive stand against 
the ‘Ring of Nestor’s’ authenticity primarily based on the observation of 
stylistic oddities.41 Agni Xenaki-Sakellariou extensively discussed some of the 
ring’s iconographical details she regarded suspicious in 1994 and critically 
commented on its typological and technical features.42 Ingo Pini’s article on 
the ‘Ring of Nestor’, written in 1998, can be read as a reply to this, for he 
referred explicitly to Xenaki-Sakellariou’s arguments, countering them by naming
new insights and evidence in support of the ring’s authenticity.43 He 
contradicted Xenaki-Sakellariou’s former observations by establishing references
to the latest data accessed only recently.", "score": 0.36622754, "raw_content":
