      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Vlachopoulos (2020) surveys the history, naming, use,
and authenticity debate of the object known as the \"Ring of Nestor\" within the
broader context of Aegean Bronze Age iconography and forgery issues. He 
reconstructs the discovery narrative and modern application of the Homeric name,
stresses that the designation \"Ring of Nestor\" is a modern construct without 
probative value for the historicity of Nestor, and reviews 20th‑century 
scholarly skepticism, particularly the tendency between roughly the 1920s and 
1950s to treat the ring as a forgery. Against this skeptical tradition, he 
analyses technical, stylistic, and iconographic features and concludes that 
there is no decisive reason to deny the ring's authenticity as a Late Bronze Age
artefact. Through comparative study of other Mycenaean signet rings and wall 
paintings, he argues that the ritual‑royal scene depicted on the ring aligns 
with Late Bronze Age elite ideology and religious practice, and any connection 
to Homeric tradition must be framed as indirect and conditional.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "Eliopoulos (2013) re‑examines the \"Ring of Nestor\" 
from an iconographic perspective with the explicit aim of contributing to the 
assessment of its authenticity and its place within Minoan‑Mycenaean art, 
situating it within wider debates on Aegean forgeries. He notes that available 
technical analyses of the ring are contradictory, which makes comparative 
iconographic study particularly important for evaluating its status. He 
highlights elements such as incurved altars and other ritual components on the 
ring that have good parallels in Bronze Age imagery and uses these 
correspondences to support the possibility that the ring is genuinely of Aegean 
Bronze Age date. Eliopoulos recalls that several generations of scholars have 
debated the authenticity of both the Nestor and Minos rings, warning that 
excluding such contested objects a priori would impoverish the evidence base for
understanding ancient societies and their material culture. While he 
acknowledges resonances between the ring's imagery and cultural practices of the
1920s, leaving conceptual space for a modern production scenario, his overall 
argument uses iconographic evidence to defend a Bronze Age attribution.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "Rimell (2020) treats the \"so‑called Ring of Nestor\"
in a broader discussion of Mycenaean signet rings and visionary or epiphanic 
religious imagery. He categorizes the ring under the heading \"Authenticity in 
question\" and assigns a tentative date of LH IB (c. 1500–1450 BC) only on the 
condition that it is genuine. He remarks that the ring was reported by Arthur 
Evans as having been discovered in the 1920s, but that its findspot and 
documentary record are highly uncertain, contributing to its longstanding 
reputation as a likely or possible forgery. Rimell incorporates the ring's 
ritual‑epiphany scene into a thematic analysis of elite religious practice, but 
explicitly warns that, because of doubts about authenticity and provenance, the 
ring should be used with caution in reconstructing Mycenaean religion. By 
classifying it as \"pseudo‑Minoan\" or \"authenticity in question,\" he reflects
a contemporary scholarly tendency to treat the object as a \"gemma dubitanda\" 
on the fringes of the genuine corpus.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
