    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "Hudler (2019) analyzes the category of \"gemmae 
dubitandae\"—Aegean Bronze Age gems and signet rings whose authenticity is 
disputed—as a case study in archaeological methodology, including the \"Ring of 
Nestor\" among these contested objects. She traces the chronology of scholarly 
positions: Nilsson initially accepted the ring as genuine in the first edition 
of \"Minoan‑Mycenaean Religion\" but, responding to increased concern over 
forgeries, explicitly withdrew this acceptance in the second edition (1950), 
reclassifying it as a suspect object. Hagen Biesantz (1954) is reported as 
taking a strongly negative stance on the ring's authenticity based on stylistic 
\"oddities,\" and Agni Xenaki‑Sakellariou (1994) expressed doubt grounded in 
iconographic, typological, and technical details. Hudler notes that Ingo Pini 
(1998) directly engaged and rejected Xenaki‑Sakellariou's arguments, using new 
comparative material to support the ring's authenticity. By synthesizing these 
opposing views, she characterizes the Ring of Nestor as a paradigmatic \"gemma 
dubitanda,\" simultaneously subjected to serious forgery accusations and still 
treated by some scholars as a potentially authentic artefact and thus a useful, 
though methodologically challenging, source.",
