───────────────────────────────── Final Report ─────────────────────────────────
                                Research Report                                 

Ring of Nestor Mycenaean discovery excavation Pylos                             

 • Evans describes the Ring of Nestor as one of a group of gold signet-rings and
   bead-seals forming a sepulchral treasure from a burial in Thisbê, Boeotia,   
   and uses this context to connect the object to early Aegean funerary customs.
   [1]                                                                          
 • In his 1925 article, Evans offers a detailed narrative of the ring’s         
   iconography, including depictions of human figures and divine beings engaged 
   in scenes he interprets as rituals related to the afterlife, and uses these  
   to argue for a Minoan conception of the ‘after-world’. [1]                   
 • Evans classifies the Ring of Nestor within the Late Bronze Age Aegean, in a  
   Minoan–Mycenaean milieu, and uses comparative evidence from other gold       
   signet-rings and seals to support this dating and cultural attribution. [1]  
 • The Nestor Bibliography entry for Evans’s Ring of Nestor study states that   
   the text used there is abridged from an article published in the Journal of  
   Hellenic Studies and categorizes it under themes related to the after-world  
   of prehistoric Greece. [2]                                                   
 • The Ashmolean Museum publication remarks that the Ring of Nestor is a gold   
   signet ring first described by Arthur Evans and notes that questions have    
   been raised about its authenticity and about how and where it was discovered.
   [3]                                                                          
 • The Ashmolean text indicates that the Ring of Nestor entered Evans’s         
   collection through his activities as a collector, implying acquisition from  
   the art market or private hands rather than from a stratigraphically recorded
   excavation. [3]                                                              
 • The Griffin Warrior article reports that the Ring of Nestor was purchased by 
   Arthur Evans and that it was supposedly found to the north of Pylos, while   
   explicitly characterizing this findspot as ‘supposed’ to signal uncertainty. 
   [4]                                                                          
 • In contrasting the Griffin Warrior tomb with earlier finds, the article      
   stresses that the Ring of Nestor’s provenance is poorly documented compared  
   to the stratigraphically excavated gold rings from the Griffin Warrior burial
   at Pylos. [4]                                                                
 • The Metropolitan Museum of Art article names the Ring of Nestor alongside the
   Phaistos Disk as one of the artifacts whose photographic and drawn           
   representations were repeatedly reproduced in early 20th-century works on the
   Greek Bronze Age. [5]                                                        
 • The Met article associates images of the Ring of Nestor with other well-known
   Aegean artifacts, such as the nautilus frieze from Pylos, to illustrate how  
   such iconic pieces contributed to forming a comparative visual corpus for    
   Bronze Age studies. [5]                                                      
 • The abstract of the technological study notes that about 165 gold objects    
   from four Mycenaean tholos tombs in a distinct Mycenaean region were examined
   to determine manufacturing techniques, alloy composition and metalworking    
   practices. [6]                                                               
 • The same technological study cites Evans’s ‘The ring of Nestor. A glimpse    
   into the Minoan after-world’ in its references, treating the Ring of Nestor  
   as a key comparative example in discussions of Aegean gold signet rings. [6] 
 • The Ring of Nestor is a gold signet ring first brought into scholarly        
   discussion by Arthur Evans, who published a study of the object in 1925 that 
   has continued to be cited in research on Aegean gold signet rings. [2][3][6] 
 • Arthur Evans classed the Ring of Nestor as a Late Bronze Age Aegean gold     
   signet ring within a Minoan–Mycenaean cultural milieu, based primarily on    
   stylistic and typological comparison with other seals and rings. [1]         
 • Arthur Evans reported that the Ring of Nestor formed part of a sepulchral    
   treasure of gold signet rings and bead-seals said to come from a burial at   
   Thisbê in Boeotia, using this claimed burial association to interpret the    
   ring in the context of early Aegean funerary customs. [1]                    
 • The Ring of Nestor does not come from a modern stratigraphically recorded    
   excavation; rather, it entered Arthur Evans’s collection via his activities  
   as a collector, and its reported findspots (e.g., a burial at Thisbê in      
   Boeotia or a location north of Pylos) are uncertain and poorly documented.   
   [1][3][4]                                                                    
 • Subsequent scholarship, as summarized by the Ashmolean Museum and other      
   authors, has raised doubts about the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor and  
   about the reliability of the reported circumstances of its discovery. [3][4] 
 • Arthur Evans interpreted the imagery on the Ring of Nestor—showing human     
   figures, divine beings and apparent ritual scenes—as evidence for a Minoan   
   conception of the afterlife and as a key source for reconstructing           
   prehistoric Greek funerary and religious beliefs. [1][2]                     
 • Images of the Ring of Nestor were widely reproduced alongside other iconic   
   Bronze Age artifacts (such as the Phaistos Disk and the nautilus frieze from 
   Pylos) in early 20th‑century publications, helping to shape the visual corpus
   and public and scholarly conceptions of Bronze Age Greece. [5]               
 • Modern technological studies of securely excavated Mycenaean gold objects    
   from tholos tombs use Evans’s study of the Ring of Nestor as a comparative   
   reference but do not include the Ring of Nestor itself among their analyzed  
   artifacts. [6]                                                               

Resolve conflict: Evans’s 1925 account presents the Ring of Nestor as part of a 
sepulchral treasure from a burial at Thisbê in Boeotia, whereas later sources   
linked to Evans’s collecting and later scholarship describe the ring as         
purchased by Evans with a supposedly northern Pylos provenance and emphasize    
uncertainty about its discovery context and authenticity.                       

 • Vlachopoulos’s chapter describes the Ring of Nestor and the ring of Minos as 
   artifacts whose discovery circumstances are "obscure" and even "legendary",  
   and notes that they have long been at the center of authenticity debates in  
   Aegean archaeology. He summarizes how doubts raised by earlier scholars have 
   been revisited in light of recent finds of comparable frescoes, gold rings,  
   ivories, and figurines, as well as detailed studies of the manufacture of    
   early Late Bronze Age gold signet rings, arguing that these lines of evidence
   now substantially support the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor. [7]        
 • In discussing Evans’s interpretation of the Ring of Nestor’s imagery,        
   Vlachopoulos focuses on the animal that Evans termed a "watch-dog" or        
   "dragon" and, using comparative iconographic material from Aegean Bronze Age 
   contexts, proposes that the figure is better understood as a scorpion serving
   an apotropaic function in the ring’s composition. [7]                        
 • Vlachopoulos explicitly situates the Ring of Nestor within the history of    
   Aegean forgeries and debates about authenticity, emphasizing that the ring’s 
   provenance narratives—including its association with a sepulchral treasure   
   from Thisbê in Boeotia and a supposed northern Pylos origin—are part of a    
   chain involving legendary discoveries, the art market, and modern collecting,
   rather than securely documented excavation contexts. [7]                     
 • The Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections article opens by stating    
   that "The so-called Nestor Ring was found in 1924 and was accepted as        
   authentic by Sir Arthur Evans whereas many other scholars condemned it as a  
   forgery," thereby documenting both the reported year of discovery and the    
   split between Evans’s acceptance and other scholars’ rejection of the ring’s 
   authenticity. [8]                                                            
 • The same article describes the Ring of Nestor as "pseudo-Minoan" and proceeds
   to analyze its scenes and motifs in comparison with Egyptian iconography,    
   framing the question of whether the ring is an ancient product of            
   Aegean–Egyptian interactions or a modern forgery drawing on Egyptian visual  
   sources. [8]                                                                 
 • The HathiTrust catalog entry gives the title of Evans’s work as "The ring of 
   Nestor; a glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of  
   gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia, by Sir Arthur Evans," 
   explicitly linking the ring to a "sepulchral treasure" of gold signet rings  
   and bead seals from Thisbê in Boeotia. [9]                                   
 • The HathiTrust record notes that Evans’s study of the Ring of Nestor is      
   associated with the Journal of Hellenic Studies and is cataloged as a formal 
   publication or offprint, supporting its status as the initial scholarly      
   report introducing the ring and the Thisbê sepulchral treasure narrative into
   academic discourse. [9]                                                      
 • The Heidelberg University Library digital portal repeats the combined title  
   "A glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a sepulchral treasure of gold     
   signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia" and lists a section        
   "Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê,        
   Boeotia. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world," indicating that Evans       
   structured the article around the description and interpretation of this     
   treasure and used the alleged Thisbê burial context as a framework for his   
   discussion. [10]                                                             
 • The Heidelberg metadata assign the digital edition of Evans’s article the DOI
   https://doi.org/10.11588/diglit.808, marking it as a citable representation  
   of the original 1925 publication. [10]                                       
 • The Academia.edu preprint "SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING 
   OF NESTOR’" states in its abstract that it presents an analysis of the       
   iconography surrounding the Ring of Nestor in the context of Aegean forgeries
   and authenticity debates, aiming to contribute to ongoing discourse regarding
   the ring’s significance in Minoan and Mycenaean art. [11]                    
 • The same abstract notes that "Generations of scholars have debated the       
   genuineness of both Nestor's and Minos' rings. Any a priori disqualification 
   of such a vast resource of emerging data is to ignore an untapped resource   
   towards our understanding of ancient societies," acknowledging a long-running
   controversy over authenticity and arguing against excluding the rings from   
   scholarly consideration. [11]                                                
 • According to the iconographic paper, re-analysis of the Ring of Nestor’s     
   imagery, including comparison with incurved altars and other motifs in Aegean
   Bronze Age art, supports its authenticity, suggesting that visual evidence   
   can counter arguments that the ring is a modern forgery. [11]                

Ring of Nestor scholarly interpretations debates function meaning Homeric Nestor

