Resolve conflict: There is a conflict between Hudler’s classification of the    
‘Ring of Nestor’ as a gemma dubitanda with problematic provenance and           
authenticity, and other scholars’ treatments that implicitly or explicitly      
accept the ring as an authentic Late Bronze Age artefact used for reconstructing
Mycenaean religious and political practice.                                     

 • Hudler describes the Ring of Nestor as a representative gemma dubitanda and  
   emphasizes that its “archaeological properties actually are – and hopefully  
   will remain – unknown,” noting the absence of excavation records and only a  
   legendary report that it was found near Dörpfeld’s “Nestor’s Pylos.” [14]    
 • Hudler states that from the 1920s onwards accusations recurred that the Ring 
   of Nestor was a modern forgery or a composite piece, contrasting this with   
   earlier acceptance by Evans. [14]                                            
 • In comparing the ring’s imagery with securely excavated Cretan–Mycenaean     
   seals, Hudler characterizes the Ring of Nestor’s scene as “übersteigert” in  
   composition and figure density and notes its close resemblance to famous     
   epiphany rings published in earlier scholarship. [14]                        
 • Hudler concludes that, unless authenticity and findspot can be verified by   
   archaeological evidence, the Ring of Nestor should be kept in the category of
   gemmae dubitandae and should not be used as a basis for reconstructing       
   Mycenaean religious practice and political ideology. [14]                    
 • Vlachopoulos’s abstract notes that the chapter aims to “document its         
   authenticity” regarding the so-called Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277) and   
   that tendencies to consider such rings as forgeries must be “completely      
   reassessed in favour of their authenticity.” [7]                             
 • The chapter emphasizes that recent discoveries of wall paintings, gold rings,
   and ivory objects provide “iconographic subjects of comparable resonance” to 
   the Ring of Nestor, reinforcing its fit within the 16th–15th century BC      
   Cretan–Mycenaean gold signet tradition. [7]                                  
 • Vlachopoulos notes that the Ring of Nestor is the “fifth largest Aegean      
   signet ring” and reinterprets Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon” as a scorpion, 
   highlighting its apotropaic function. [7]                                    
 • Vlachopoulos argues that a 1920s forger would have struggled to create a     
   scene that so closely matches iconographic and technical details only        
   recognized thanks to later discoveries, implying that the Ring of Nestor’s   
   coherence with these comparanda is an argument against forgery. [7]          
 • Vlachopoulos concedes that the ring’s discovery and collection history are   
   “obscure – even legendary – circumstances,” but he argues that this should   
   not in itself disqualify the object from being used as evidence if its       
   unexcavated status is explicitly acknowledged. [7]                           
 • Eliopoulos states that placing the Ring of Nestor in the context of Aegean   
   forgeries and authenticity debates allows for a reassessment of its          
   “significance in Minoan and Mycenaean art” via close iconographic comparison.
   [11]                                                                         
 • According to Eliopoulos, the connection between incurved altars on the Ring  
   of Nestor and similar motifs on securely provenanced artefacts “supports its 
   authenticity,” because these shared features are difficult to account for as 
   a modern forger’s invention. [11]                                            
 • Eliopoulos observes that “generations of scholars have debated the           
   genuineness of both Nestor’s and Minos’ rings” and warns against “a priori   
   disqualification” of unexcavated objects without considering their           
   iconographic and technical evidence. [11]                                    
 • Eliopoulos concludes that, while issues regarding the ring’s findspot and    
   collection history persist, its iconography and technical traits “more       
   closely” align with Late Bronze Age Cretan–Mycenaean seal traditions than    
   with known modern forgeries, justifying its cautious use in studying         
   religious and power symbolism. [11]                                          
 • Pini’s abstract notes that the article presents “new technical observations” 
   that “serve to support the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor,” indicating   
   that production techniques and wear patterns are consistent with ancient     
   manufacture. [18]                                                            
 • Pini reports that the Ring of Nestor was said to have been found “near Pylos”
   in 1924 and to have entered a museum collection via an antiquities dealer,   
   and he emphasizes the absence of controlled excavation, concluding that its  
   provenance remains uncertain. [18]                                           
 • Pini compares the ring’s multi-figure religious scene to that of the Isopata 
   epiphany ring and other LM II / LH IIB–IIIA1 signets, and he argues that     
   their compositional and stylistic similarities indicate that the Ring of     
   Nestor belongs to this high-status Cretan–Mycenaean signet tradition. [18]   
 • Pini concludes by rejecting the notion that the Ring of Nestor is a modern   
   forgery and recommends that, with its lack of archaeological context clearly 
