   interpreted as depicting a religious or mythological scene in which a griffin
   sits on a table altar understood as a wooden, portable sacrificial           
   installation, with the griffin functioning as a supernatural guardian or     
   divine symbol; this reading downplays interpretations of the ring as a scene 
   of royal investiture. [22]                                                   
 • In the study Ancient Gods, the Ring of Nestor is used as visual evidence for 
   the existence of a young male divinity associated with a goddess in Crete and
   the wider Aegean, and its scene is interpreted as an interaction between a   
   goddess and a youthful male god or his symbol, supporting a goddess–youth    
   divine pair rather than a depiction of a mortal worshipper or a purely royal 
   investiture. [25]                                                            
 • Across these scholarly interpretations, the Ring of Nestor is variously      
   understood as (1) a paradigmatic divine epiphany scene with ritual functions,
   (2) a record of ecstatic, visionary religious experience, (3) a              
   representation of afterlife or underworld beliefs in a funerary context, and 
   (4) a mythological or cultic image involving a goddess and a young male      
   divinity; these positions disagree especially over whether the ring primarily
   encodes theophany and ritual experience, funerary eschatology, or specific   
   mythological content. [20][21][22][23][25]                                   

"Ring of Nestor" Pylos Homeric Nestor scholarly debates naming justification    

 • Vlachopoulos notes that the "Ring of Nestor" measures 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm and 
   describes it as the fifth largest Aegean gold signet ring known. [7]         
 • Through comparison with other Late Bronze Age gold signet rings, Vlachopoulos
   argues that the workmanship and style of the "Ring of Nestor" are consistent 
   with Mycenaean goldsmithing, supporting its authenticity as a Late Bronze Age
   artefact. [7]                                                                
 • Vlachopoulos summarises that Nilsson and Biesantz had previously classified  
   the "Ring of Nestor" and "Ring of Minos" as modern fakes, often linking them 
   to Emile or Georges Gilliéron. [7]                                           
 • Vlachopoulos explains that the designation "Ring of Nestor" emerged in the   
   early twentieth century when Evans and Gilliéron popularised the ring in     
   drawings and publications, associating it with Nestor of Pylos without any   
   inscription naming the owner. [7]                                            
 • In his iconographic analysis, Vlachopoulos interprets the scene on the "Ring 
   of Nestor" as depicting ritual activity connected with elite ideology,       
   arguing that such imagery can inform our understanding of Mycenaean concepts 
   of kingship that later resonate with Homeric descriptions. [7]               
 • The Nestor Bibliography entry for A. G. Vlachopoulos lists: "The Ring of     
   Nestor and the Quest for Authenticity," in F. Blakolmer (ed.), Current       
   Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography (Aegis 18, 2020), pp.  
   223–252. [26]                                                                
 • The Nestor Bibliography summarises Vlachopoulos's chapter as addressing the  
   authenticity of the "Ring of Nestor" and its role in Aegean iconography,     
   including discussions of its naming, alleged findspot, and iconographic      
   interpretation. [26]                                                         
 • Gere remarks that the "Ring of Nestor" was repeatedly cited in debates about 
   forgeries associated with the Gilliéron workshop, in part because its        
   reported find circumstances at Pylos were vague and its style was seen as    
   anomalous. [27]                                                              
 • Gere cites Nilsson and subsequent scholars who proposed that the "Ring of    
   Minos" and "Ring of Nestor" may have been fabricated by the Gilliéron        
   workshop, noting that such objects conveniently matched contemporary         
   expectations of artefacts linked to Homeric heroes. [27]                     
 • Gere argues that the naming of artefacts such as the "Ring of Minos" and     
   "Ring of Nestor" reflects a romantic desire to discover tangible survivals of
   Homeric and legendary figures and that these names shaped public perception  
   more than the underlying archaeological evidence. [27]                       
 • Gere concludes that the label "Ring of Nestor" is a modern construct and     
   warns that its evocative name should not be taken as evidence for the        
   historicity of Nestor in the absence of independent archaeological or textual
   corroboration. [27]                                                          
 • Palmer writes: "The ‘Ring of Minos’ (AM 1938.1110) and ‘Ring of Nestor’      
   (VI.277) have both been denounced as fakes by Nilsson (1950: 42–49) and      
   Biesantz (1954: 115–119)…" [28]                                              
 • Palmer comments that naming these rings after figures like Minos and Nestor  
   reflects a broader trend among early twentieth‑century scholars and          
   collectors to seek direct Homeric connections in the archaeological record,  
   despite the absence of inscriptions identifying owners. [28]                 
 • Palmer explains that she excludes the "Ring of Nestor" from her dataset of   
   Late Bronze Age religious glyptic motifs because its authenticity has been   
   questioned and including it could distort the analysis if it is indeed a     
   modern forgery. [28]                                                         
 • Palmer notes that later scholars have defended the authenticity of the "Ring 
   of Nestor" and observes that, if accepted as genuine, its iconography might  
   portray a cultic scene involving a ruler, which could be compared with       
   Homeric narratives about Nestor's religious and political functions. [28]    
 • Nilsson writes that the "Ring of Nestor" "seem[s] rather to be [a] modern    
   product", basing this judgement on differences in style and workmanship      
   compared with other Mycenaean signet rings. [29]                             
 • Nilsson argues that Evans's account of the discovery and subsequent history  
   of the "Ring of Nestor" lacks verifiable archaeological documentation,       
   weakening any claim that it comes from Mycenaean Pylos or has a concrete     
   connection to a historical Nestor. [29]                                      
 • Nilsson criticises the practice of naming prehistoric artefacts after        
   mythological figures, stating that labels like "Ring of Nestor" encourage    
   romanticised interpretations and can mislead readers about the historical    
   reality behind the objects. [29]                                             
 • The volume Sir Arthur Evans and Minoan Crete reproduces Evans's drawing of   
   the "Ring of Nestor" from his Knossos notebook (Ashmolean Museum) and notes  
   that Evans stated the ring was found in a large beehive tomb at "Nestor's    
   Pylos". [30]                                                                 
 • The same study analyses Evans's practice of assigning evocative names to     
   artefacts, explaining that he connected objects like the "Ring of Nestor" and
   "Ring of Minos" to Homeric heroes as part of a broader effort to craft a     
   compelling narrative of continuity between Bronze Age sites and epic         
   tradition. [30]                                                              
 • The discussion in Sir Arthur Evans and Minoan Crete observes that the        
   attribution of the "Ring of Nestor" to a heroic owner rests on Evans's       
   interpretive framework and not on any inscription or direct evidence of the  
   ring's ancient possessor. [30]                                               
 • The designation "Ring of Nestor" is a modern scholarly and popular label     
   created in the early twentieth century—especially through Arthur Evans and   
   Gilliéron’s drawings and narratives—and is not based on any ancient          
   inscription or direct evidence identifying the ring’s owner. [7][27][28][30] 
 • Scholars such as Nilsson, Gere, and Palmer criticise the label "Ring of      
   Nestor" as part of an early twentieth‑century romantic practice of naming    
   prehistoric artefacts after Homeric or mythological figures, arguing that    
   such evocative labels project narratives of direct Homeric connections onto  
   the material and can mislead about historical or archaeological realities.   
   [27][28][29]                                                                 
 • Nilsson and Biesantz argued that the "Ring of Nestor" is likely a modern     
   forgery, possibly produced in the Gilliéron workshop, citing stylistic and   
   technical discrepancies from securely provenanced Mycenaean signet rings and 
   the weak documentation of its reported discovery at Pylos. [7][27][28][29]   
 • Subsequent scholarship, particularly A. G. Vlachopoulos, has argued on       
   technical and stylistic grounds that the "Ring of Nestor" is an authentic    
   Late Bronze Age Aegean gold signet ring, countering earlier claims that it is
   a modern forgery. [7][28]                                                    
 • Because the authenticity of the "Ring of Nestor" has been seriously          
   contested, some analysts, such as Palmer, exclude it from quantitative       
   studies of Late Bronze Age religious glyptic motifs to avoid skewing results 
   if it were in fact a modern forgery. [28]                                    
 • The provenance of the "Ring of Nestor" as coming from a beehive tomb at      
   "Nestor’s Pylos" rests primarily on Arthur Evans’s notebook accounts and has 
   been criticised as lacking independent, verifiable archaeological            
   documentation. [29][30]                                                      
 • If the "Ring of Nestor" is accepted as authentic, Vlachopoulos and Palmer    
   argue that its iconography depicts elite or royal ritual scenes that can     
   inform reconstructions of Mycenaean kingship ideology and religious practice,
   which may resonate indirectly with later Homeric portrayals of Nestor’s royal
   and cultic roles without demonstrating a direct personal connection. [7][28] 
 • Gere and Nilsson argue that the name "Ring of Nestor" should not be treated  
   as evidence for the historicity of the Homeric Nestor or for a direct link   
   between the object and a historical king of Pylos, in the absence of         
   independent archaeological or textual corroboration. [27][29]                
 • The scholarly reception of the "Ring of Nestor" has been shaped significantly
   by Arthur Evans’s authority and his narrative of Mycenaean–Homeric           
   continuity, which helped establish the ring as an iconic object despite      
   contested provenance and authenticity. [27][30]                              
 • Vlachopoulos’s study "The Ring of Nestor and the Quest for Authenticity" is  
   positioned within research on Late Bronze Age Aegean iconography and         
   religion, treating the ring’s authenticity, naming, alleged findspot, and    
   imagery as case studies for broader questions about Mycenaean cult and royal 
   symbolism. [26]                                                              

Resolve conflict: Disagreement over the authenticity of the "Ring of Nestor":   
Nilsson and later critics regard it as likely a modern forgery, whereas         
Vlachopoulos and some recent scholars argue that it is an authentic Late Bronze 
Age artefact.                                                                   

 • Vlachopoulos frames his study as placing the Ring of Nestor within the       
   broader problem of Aegean Bronze Age iconography and forgeries, reviewing its
   discovery history, naming, and use, and concluding from technical, stylistic,
   and iconographic analysis that there is no decisive reason to deny its status
   as a genuine Bronze Age artefact. [7]                                        
 • In discussing the ring's modern name, Vlachopoulos stresses that the         
   designation "Ring of Nestor" was created in modern scholarship in relation to
   the Homeric figure Nestor and does not derive from any ancient inscription or
   documentation associating the artefact with a historical person. [7]         
 • Vlachopoulos notes that, especially between the 1920s and 1950s, several     
   scholars treated the Ring of Nestor as a forgery, forming a skeptical        
   tradition that he re‑examines by comparing the ring to securely provenanced  
   Mycenaean signet rings and wall paintings. [7]                               
 • Through iconographic comparison, Vlachopoulos identifies the ring's scene as 
   a ritual‑royal context consistent with Late Bronze Age elite ideology, using 
   this match in themes and style to argue against its fabrication as a modern  
   pastiche. [7]                                                                
 • Vlachopoulos explicitly states that any attempt to connect the Ring of Nestor
   to Homeric tradition must acknowledge that the ring's name and interpretation
   are modern and that the object cannot serve as direct historical proof of    
   Nestor's existence or possessions. [7]                                       
 • Eliopoulos introduces his article by stating that its purpose is to address  
   the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor and its place in Minoan‑Mycenaean art 
   through iconographic analysis, recognizing the ring's position in debates    
   over Aegean forgeries. [11]                                                  
 • Eliopoulos observes that existing technical studies of the Ring of Nestor    
   yield contradictory results, which he cites as a reason to prioritize        
   iconographic comparisons to Bronze Age imagery when assessing authenticity.  
   [11]                                                                         
 • In his iconographic analysis, Eliopoulos points to the presence of incurved  
   altars on the Ring of Nestor and notes that such features have well‑attested 
   counterparts in Bronze Age Aegean imagery, using these parallels as support  
   for the ring's authenticity. [11]                                            
 • Eliopoulos acknowledges that aspects of the ring's imagery resonate with     
   cultural practices of the 1920s, a point he raises to show that even objects 
   with potentially modern stylistic influences can still warrant careful       
   analysis rather than outright dismissal. [11]                                
 • Eliopoulos remarks that several generations of scholars have debated the     
   authenticity of the Nestor and Minos rings and cautions that pre‑emptively   
   excluding such contested artefacts could lead to loss of potential           
   information about ancient societies and their material culture. [11]         
 • Rimell categorizes the "so‑called Ring of Nestor" under the heading          
   "Authenticity in question" and notes an approximate dating to LH IB, around  
   1500–1450 BC, explicitly conditioned on the ring being genuine. [31]         
 • Rimell writes that Arthur Evans reported the ring as having been discovered  
   in the 1920s but that the reported findspot and surviving documentation are  
   highly uncertain, a situation that has contributed to the ring being long    
   regarded as a possible forgery. [31]                                         
 • In his thematic discussion of epiphany imagery, Rimell includes the Ring of  
   Nestor among the material but explicitly warns that, due to unresolved       
   questions about authenticity and provenance, it should be used with caution  
   when reconstructing Mycenaean religious beliefs and practices. [31]          
 • By referring to the Ring of Nestor as "pseudo‑Minoan" and placing it in an   
   "authenticity in question" category, Rimell reflects a scholarly practice of 
   treating such objects as "gemmae dubitandae"—items whose stylistic traits and
   uncertain provenance raise doubts about authenticity. [31]                   
 • Hudler notes that Nilsson, in the second edition of "Minoan‑Mycenaean        
   Religion" published in 1950, reclassified the Ring of Nestor from an accepted
   genuine artefact to a "suspect object" in response to growing concerns in the
   scholarly community about Aegean forgeries. [14]                             
 • Hudler reports that Hagen Biesantz, in a 1954 publication, argued against the
   authenticity of the Ring of Nestor by pointing to stylistic oddities that led
   him to regard it as a modern forgery. [14]                                   
 • Hudler explains that Agni Xenaki‑Sakellariou's 1994 study raised doubts about
   the Ring of Nestor based on a combination of iconographic details, typology, 
   and technical characteristics, concluding that the object might be a forgery.
   [14]                                                                         
 • Hudler states that Ingo Pini, writing in 1998, directly confronted           
   Xenaki‑Sakellariou's criticisms of the Ring of Nestor and used newly         
   available comparative material to argue that the ring should be considered   
   authentic. [14]                                                              
 • Hudler classifies the Ring of Nestor among "gemmae dubitandae," defining it  
   as an artefact that has been seriously challenged as a possible forgery yet  
   remains in scholarly use by some authors who treat it as potentially genuine,
   making the authenticity debate itself a subject of methodological reflection.
   [14]                                                                         
 • According to the ResearchGate abstract, the study "The Pseudo‑Minoan Nestor  
   Ring and Its Egyptian Iconography" states that the Nestor Ring was discovered
   in 1924, accepted as genuine by Arthur Evans, and later regarded as a forgery
   by many other scholars, summarizing the divided views on its authenticity.   
   [32]                                                                         
 • The same abstract emphasizes connections between the ring's iconography and  
   Egyptian imagery, suggesting that if the ring is authentic, its motifs could 
   serve as evidence for cultural interactions between the Mycenaean world and  
   Egypt. [32]                                                                  
 • By using the term "Pseudo‑Minoan" in the title, the Egyptian iconography     
   study signals that the Nestor Ring exhibits stylistic traits that differ from
   canonical Minoan works and acknowledges its contested authenticity status    
   while still engaging with its imagery. [32]                                  

