   [40]                                                                         
 • 馆方描述“Ring of                                                             
   Minos”戒面上刻有复杂的宗教场景，包括神祇、树木与建筑元素，反映典型的米诺斯宗 
   教图像学。 [40]                                                              
 • Smithsonian Magazine                                                         
   报道称，狮鹫战士墓出土的金戒指由多片金板制成，并描绘极其细致的场景和源自米诺 
   斯神话的图像学。 [39]                                                        
 • 网站指出，金印戒指作为随葬品，与墓主人社会身份和权力象征相关，其米诺斯式宗教 
   图像与墓主人迈锡尼身份一起被视为迈锡尼‑米诺斯文化互动的关键证据之一。 [37]   
 • Griffin Warrior Tomb                                                         
   项目网站称在该墓葬中发现了四枚金印戒指，这些戒指装饰有米诺斯仪式场景。 [37]  
 • 伊拉克利翁考古博物馆的展品说明称“Ring of                                     
   Minos”是一枚卓越的金戒指，可能是迄今在克里特发现的最大金戒指。 [40]          
 • 报道指出这些戒指由多层金片制成，戒面刻有复杂的宗教或神话场景，展示高超工艺并 
   与米诺斯神话题材密切相关。 [38]                                              
 • 辛辛那提大学杂志报道，狮鹫战士墓发现的四枚金印戒指饰有高度细致的米诺斯图像学 
   ，被考古团队视为迈锡尼‑米诺斯互动的最佳事例之一。 [38]                       
 • 该网站将狮鹫战士墓描述为早期迈锡尼精英男性墓葬，内含大量金器、武器和象牙等奢 
   侈品。 [37]                                                                  
 • 论文强调椭圆构图在米诺斯微型艺术中具有重要性，并认为戒指椭圆形边界与内部宗教 
   场景的结合在视觉上引导观者聚焦核心仪式行为。 [36]                            

Ring of Nestor archaeological context dating Mycenaean chronology               

 • The grave of the Griffin Warrior, an undisturbed single burial of an adult   
   male with rich grave goods, was discovered in 2015 approximately 150 m       
   southwest of the Palace of Nestor at Pylos. [12]                             
 • On the basis of pottery, metal vessels, and ivory artifacts, the grave is    
   dated by the authors to Late Helladic IIA, roughly the early to middle 15th  
   century BC, predating the main construction phase of the Palace of Nestor.   
   [12]                                                                         
 • Four gold signet rings of exceptional workmanship were found in the grave,   
   bearing scenes of Minoan-style religious rituals and bull-leaping. [12]      
 • The authors argue that these signet rings functioned as symbols of authority 
   and markers of elite identity in the early Mycenaean world. [12]             
 • The grave of the Griffin Warrior is described as a rectangular shaft grave   
   with a cover of stone slabs and a stepped dromos on its west side. [41]      
 • The four gold signet rings are numbered Ring 1–4 according to their findspots
   and order of discovery, and their positions relative to the skeleton suggest 
   that at least one ring originally was worn on the left hand. [41]            
 • In the discussion of chronology, the grave is placed in the transition from  
   LH I to LH IIA and is considered slightly later than the Shaft Graves of     
   Grave Circle A at Mycenae but still pre-palatial in terms of Mycenaean palace
   development. [41]                                                            
 • Iconographic analysis interprets the scenes with goddess figures, offerings, 
   trees, and pillars on the rings as "Minoanized" religious imagery occurring  
   within a clearly Mycenaean funerary context. [41]                            
 • A report on the discovery notes that the grave, located near the Palace of   
   Nestor at ancient Pylos, was intact and unplundered, a very rare             
   circumstance. [42]                                                           
 • The article states that four solid-gold rings were found in the grave, one   
   decorated with a Cretan bull-jumping scene, and remarks that this number of  
   gold rings is unparalleled in known Mycenaean burials. [42]                  
 • The initial estimate given for the grave's date is around 1500 BC, described 
   as belonging to the Early Mycenaean period and compared with the Shaft Graves
   at Mycenae. [42]                                                             
 • According to the report, excavators suggest that the finds from the grave    
   will change our understanding of the formation of the Mycenaean kingdom and  
   its relations with Crete. [42]                                               
 • A feature article locates the grave of the Griffin Warrior in an olive grove 
   west of the hill of the Palace of Nestor, near modern Chora. [43]            
 • The grave is said to date to a generation or two before the palace at Pylos  
   was built, corresponding to the Late Helladic IIA phase. [43]                
 • The article notes that the gold artifacts from the grave include four gold   
   rings engraved with elaborate scenes of combat, animals, and religious       
   rituals, demonstrating the warrior's elite status and connections with Cretan
   culture. [43]                                                                
 • Citing Davis and Stocker, the feature suggests that the jewelry and other    
   luxury items from the grave indicate that the buried man played an important 
   role in the emerging Mycenaean political hierarchy that eventually produced  
   the Palace of Nestor and the kings described in Homer. [43]                  
 • The National Archaeological Museum describes the Palace of Nestor at Pylos as
   the archaeological counterpart of the kingdom of Nestor in Homeric epic and a
   key site for studying Mycenaean palatial society. [44]                       
 • The exhibition announcement highlights the recently excavated grave of the   
   Griffin Warrior and its rich assemblage of gold and precious stone objects as
   evidence for the wealth and power of the early Mycenaean elite at Pylos. [44]
 • The exhibition focuses on palace architecture, Linear B tablets, and         
   high-quality artifacts to reconstruct the political and religious            
   organization of the kingdom of Pylos, situating items such as gold rings     
   within a broader system of royal and ritual objects. [44]                    
 • The museum notes that discoveries such as the Griffin Warrior's grave have   
   renewed discussion of Nestor and his kingdom in the Homeric tradition by     
   juxtaposing material evidence from Pylos with epic narratives. [44]          
 • The Ring of Nestor (one of four exceptional gold signet rings) comes from the
   undisturbed single burial known as the grave of the Griffin Warrior, a       
   rectangular shaft grave with stone-slab cover and a stepped dromos, located  
   approximately 150 m southwest of the Palace of Nestor at Pylos in an olive   
   grove west of the palace hill. [12][41][42][43]                              
 • The gold signet rings from the Griffin Warrior grave, including the Ring of  
   Nestor, are explicitly associated with an early Mycenaean funerary context   
   but bear iconography of Minoan-style religious rituals and bull-leaping,     
   indicating interaction between Minoan imagery and Mycenaean elite and royal  
   symbolism. [12][41][42][43]                                                  
 • On typological grounds (analysis of pottery, metal vessels, and ivory), the  
   Griffin Warrior grave—and therefore the Ring of Nestor—are dated to Late     
   Helladic IIA, in the transitional phase from LH I to LH IIA, roughly the     
   early to middle 15th century BC, a generation or two before the construction 
   of the Palace of Nestor and slightly later than the Shaft Graves of Grave    
   Circle A at Mycenae. [12][41][42][43]                                        
 • Within its early Mycenaean archaeological context, the Ring of Nestor, as    
   part of the set of four exceptional gold signet rings from the Griffin       
   Warrior grave, is interpreted by excavators and museum presentations as a    
   symbol of authority and a marker of elite identity embedded in emerging      
   Mycenaean royal and ritual object systems, and as relevant to discussions of 
   the formation of the Mycenaean kingdom at Pylos and its Homeric Nestor       
   tradition. [12][42][43][44]                                                  
 • The find positions of the four gold signet rings in the Griffin Warrior grave
   suggest that at least one ring, potentially the Ring of Nestor, was          
   originally worn on the left hand of the deceased, supporting its             
   interpretation as a personal marker of identity in addition to its role as an
   elite symbol. [41]                                                           

Ring of Nestor significance Mycenaean Greece Homeric tradition archaeology      
literature                                                                      

 • “In May 2015, a University of Cincinnati team unexpectedly discovered a large
   stone-built tomb of Late Helladic IIA date near Tholos Tomb IV on the first  
   day of renewed excavations at the Palace of Nestor, Pylos. Hundreds of       
   artifacts of gold, silver, bronze, ivory, and semiprecious stones were found 
   with the body of a single male, 30–35 years old, dubbed the ‘Griffin         
   Warrior.’” [45]                                                              
 • “Among the gold artifacts were four signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual
   scenes. Here we discuss the excavation of the grave, describe the rings, and 
   consider the significance of the rings’ iconography for the Mycenaeans who   
   buried them.” [45]                                                           
 • 文章摘要概述，作者通过详细描绘戒指上的祭祀场景（女神、祭坛、树木、船只等）， 
   探讨这些图像如何反映迈锡尼精英对米诺斯宗教与权力象征的接受与再语境化。 [45]  
 • 作者指出这批戒指的米诺斯风格与埋葬地点——尼斯托耳宫附近——共同表明晚期赫拉迪克 
   早期皮洛斯精英在政治与宗教观念上深度参与东地中海网络，对后来的迈锡尼宫殿社会 
   具有基础性意义。 [45]                                                        
 • “In May 2015, a University of Cincinnati team unexpectedly discovered a large
   stone-built tomb of Late Helladic IIA date near Tholos Tomb IV on the first  
   day of renewed excavations at the Palace of Nestor, Pylos.” [12]             
 • “Hundreds of artifacts of gold, silver, bronze, ivory, and semiprecious      
   stones were found with the body of a single male, 30–35 years old, dubbed the
   ‘Griffin Warrior.’” [12]                                                     
 • “Many of the grave goods were manufactured in the Minoan world. Among the    
   gold artifacts were four signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes.”  
   [12]                                                                         
 • “Here we discuss the excavation of the grave, describe the rings, and        
   consider the significance of the rings’ iconography for the Mycenaeans who   
   buried them.” [12]                                                           
 • “The age of Homer was an age of heroes—Agamemnon, the king of Mycenae,       
   Odysseus, the king of Ithaca, and Nestor, the king of Pylos, among           
   others—whose deeds are chronicled in the Iliad and the Odyssey.” [46]        
 • “Many archaeologists believe that Homer’s tales, despite being composed 500  
   or more years after the Late Bronze Age events they describe, had roots in a 
   real past. ‘There’s always a kernel of truth to stories handed down from     
   generation to generation,’ says archaeologist Jack Davis.” [46]              
 • “Excavations at Pylos, and at sites all across mainland Greece, have provided
   a great deal of evidence of the Mycenaeans in their prime… they were tied    
   into a world that encompassed most of the eastern Mediterranean, including   
   ancient Egypt, the city-states of the Near East, and the islands of the      
   Mediterranean.” [46]                                                         
 • 文中以狮鹫战士墓中的奢华随葬品（包括带有米诺斯风格图像的金印戒）为例，说明皮 
   洛斯精英在物质文化和象征体系上与米诺斯世界深度交织，并被视为理解荷马时代“尼斯
   托耳”形象背后历史现实的重要证据。 [46]                                       
 • “When University of Cincinnati researchers uncovered the tomb of a Bronze Age
   warrior — left untouched for more than 3,500 years and packed with a         
   spectacular array of precious jewelry, weapons and riches — the discovery was
   hailed by experts as ‘the find of a lifetime.’” [38]                         
 • “New understandings of the artifacts — particularly the discovery of four    
   golden rings — and the insights they provide to the origins of Greek         
   civilization may prove to be the team's next big discovery.” [38]            
 • 文章强调这批金戒在图像学上的复杂性：一枚戒指上的女神持镜场景与墓中发现的青铜 
   镜相互呼应，被解读为反映精英身份与宗教仪式的象征体系。 [38]                  
 • 文中引用研究者观点，指出这些戒指及其它米诺斯风格物品表明早期迈锡尼精英不仅是 
   战士也可能是祭司或统治者，在构建后来的希腊文明叙事中具有重要地位。 [38]      
 • “In May 2015 on the first day of renewed excavations at the palace, the team 
   unexpectedly discovered a large, stone tomb. Hundreds of artifacts were found
   along with the body of a single male, the ‘Griffin Warrior.’” [47]           
 • “On the floor of the grave lay the skeleton of a single adult male surrounded
   by an enormous hoard of wealth… Gold cups rested on the warrior’s chest and  
   stomach and by his right side were hundreds of carnelian, amethyst, amber,   
   and gold beads.” [47]                                                        
 • “A gold chain and a pendent, dozens of seal-stones carved with intricate     
   designs, and four gold rings glittered beside him. A plaque of ivory with a  
   representation of a griffon in a rocky landscape lay between the man’s legs. 
   Nearby was a bronze mirror with an ivory handle.” [47]                       
 • 文中说明该墓葬年代处于“the very beginning of Mycenaean                       
   civilization”，并指出随葬物品多为米诺斯风格或克里特制造，强调这位领袖在迈锡尼
   文明形成初期的政治与文化角色。 [47]                                          
 • “The Cup of Nestor from Shaft Grave IV at                                    
   Mycenae”一文指出，迈锡尼墓葬中的某些器物因后来的荷马史诗而获得新的名称与叙事 
   意义，强调物质文化与文学传统之间并非简单对应，而是通过记忆和再解释形成的联系 
   。 [48]                                                                      
 • 文中提到“尼斯托耳之杯”与荷马文本中“尼斯托耳之杯”仅有“minor                   
   similarities”且缺乏明确考古平行物，用以警示将特定文物直接认定为史诗中具体物件
   的风险。 [48]                                                                
 • 作者分析迈锡尼精英墓葬中的器物组合与图像如何构建身份与权威，并探讨后世如何通 
   过神话将这些物品与著名英雄（如尼斯托耳）关联。 [48]                          
 • 论文强调在解读任何“尼斯托耳”命名器物时需要谨慎区分考古证据与文学投射，这一方 
   法论可直接应用于关于“尼斯托耳之戒”的讨论。 [48]                              
 • Scholarly discussions of artifacts labeled as belonging to Nestor (such as a 
   hypothetical 'Ring of Nestor') emphasize that researchers must carefully     
   distinguish archaeological evidence from later literary projection when      
   relating Mycenaean objects to Homeric figures. [48]                          
 • The scholarly article on the Cup of Nestor argues that some Mycenaean grave  
   goods acquire names and narrative meanings from later Homeric epic, and that 
   the relationship between material culture and the Homeric tradition is       
   mediated by memory and reinterpretation rather than simple one-to-one        
   identification of specific artifacts with epic objects. [48]                 
 • The Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, dated to Late   
   Helladic IIA and containing four gold signet rings decorated with Minoan     
   ritual scenes along with many other Minoan-style or Minoan-made grave goods, 
   is interpreted by scholars as evidence that early Mycenaean elites at Pylos  
   were deeply entangled in eastern Mediterranean religious and political       
   networks and that such elites played a foundational role in the development  
   of later Mycenaean palatial society. [12][38][45][46][47][48]                
 • The iconography of the four gold signet rings from the Griffin Warrior       
   grave—featuring Minoan-style ritual scenes with goddesses, altars, trees, and
   ships, and in one case a goddess holding a mirror that echoes the bronze     
   mirror found in the tomb—is interpreted as part of an elite symbolic system  
   expressing religious ritual and authority and showing Mycenaean appropriation
   and re-contextualization of Minoan religious imagery. [12][38][45][46][47]   
 • Scholars and synthesizing accounts use the archaeological discoveries at the 
   Palace of Nestor and the Griffin Warrior grave near Pylos as key evidence in 
   reconstructing the historical context behind the Homeric figure of Nestor and
   the wider Homeric age, while stressing that Homeric tales likely preserve a  
   kernel of historical truth filtered through generations of storytelling.     
   [46][47]                                                                     

